#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Stream Stoxkart order updates from its order websocket into the Redis hash `stoxkart:orders:orders`, and keep streaming until stopped.

    bin/stoxkart/order_updates
    redis-cli HGETALL stoxkart:orders:orders
    redis-cli XRANGE stoxkart:order-updates:stream - + COUNT 5

**The socket.** Stoxkart's API documentation mentions only a postback URL for order status. Stoxkart's own trading website instead receives order updates on `wss://openapi-v2.stoxkart.com/websocket/v2/connect`, and on 2026-09-15 that socket accepted the API app's own session:

1. `POST https://openapi-v2.stoxkart.com/websocket/authenticate` with the headers `x-client-id` (the `ucc_code` setting), `x-access-token` (the API access token), `x-platform: api` and `x-api-key` answers `{"message": "Authentication successful, server ready to accept WS", "status": "success", "data": {"ClientId": ..., "RequestId": ...}}`. The same call with `x-platform: web` is refused with `AuthorizationError`, so this is the API platform's access, not the website's.
2. The socket is opened at `wss://openapi-v2.stoxkart.com/websocket/v2/connect?x-client-id=<client>&x-platform=api&RequestId=<RequestId>`.
3. `{"type": "heartbeat"}` is sent on connecting and every 30 seconds, as Stoxkart's website does. Updates arrive as JSON text.

Stoxkart keeps one order socket per client. A new connection closes the older one with a close reason containing `new incoming connection`, so this script and a logged-in Stoxkart website or app knock each other off. When that happens here the script waits five minutes before reclaiming the socket, so a person using the website is not fought over every few seconds.

**What an update holds** was first seen on 2026-09-15, when three KWIL orders sent after the close were rejected: an update arrived within a second of each order, carrying `client_id`, `user_id`, `order_id`, `exch_order_id`, `order_timestamp` (blank on a rejection), `variety`, `exchange`, `trading_symbol`, `symbol`, `token`, `segment`, `order_type`, `transaction_type`, `validity`, `product`, `quantity`, `disclose_quantity`, `disclose_quantity_remaining`, `price`, `trigger_price`, `traded_quantity`, `pending_quantity`, `order_status`, `reason`, `expiry_date`, `strike_price`, `option_type` and `lot_size`, every value a string. No update arrived when two after-market orders were placed the same evening, which only `bin/stoxkart/orders` recorded, but one arrived for each when they were cancelled, with `order_status` `AMO CANCELLED`. Updates for open, partly filled and filled orders have not been seen yet. Every JSON object received is logged at INFO with its first 300 characters.

**Keys.** An object carrying an `order_id` is an order update:

- It is merged into `stoxkart:orders:orders`, the hash `bin/stoxkart/orders` also writes, keyed by `order_id`, as `{"observed_at": <epoch>, "source": "websocket", "order": <normalized order>, "data": <the update as Stoxkart sent it>, "variety": <the order's variety>}`. `variety` is what the REST API's cancel endpoint builds Stoxkart's cancel path from. Stoxkart's socket reported `NORMAL` for after-market orders whose order book row said `AMO`, so an `AMO` or `BO` variety already stored for the order is kept, a status beginning with `AMO` means `AMO`, and only otherwise is the update's own `variety` used. A websocket update always replaces its entry; the poller replaces an entry only when it was observed before the poll's request was sent. The hash expires at 06:00 IST, moved to the next 06:00 by every write.
- It is appended to the Redis Stream `stoxkart:order-updates:stream` as an `update` field holding `{"timestamp": "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS.ffffff", "order": <normalized order>, "data": <the update>}`, capped at about 20,000 entries, which `bin/stoxkart/persist_orders` writes to `stoxkart.order_updates` and `bin/unified/order_updates` combines.

Any other object - an acknowledgement, a heartbeat reply - is logged and not stored.

**The normalized order** has the fields `bin/stoxkart/orders` builds, read from the order book's field names or, where an update may name a field differently, from the likeliest alternative:

| field | from the update |
| --- | --- |
| `order_id`, `exchange_order_id`, `parent_order_id` | `order_id`, `exch_order_id` or `exchange_order_id`, `parent_order_id` |
| `status`, `status_message` | `status` or `order_status` on the shared vocabulary, `reason`, `rejection_reason` or `status_message` |
| `id`, `instrument_token`, `tradingsymbol`, `exchange` | `exchange:tradingsymbol`, `token` or `instrument_token`, `symbol_description` for a contract whose description names it and otherwise `symbol` or `trading_symbol`, `exchange` |
| `transaction_type`, `product`, `order_type`, `validity` | `action` or `transaction_type`, `product_type` or `product`, `order_type`, `validity`, on the shared vocabulary |
| `quantity`, `filled_quantity`, `pending_quantity`, `disclosed_quantity` | `quantity`, `traded_quantity` or `filled_quantity`, `pending_quantity`, `disclose_quantity` or `disclosed_quantity` |
| `price`, `trigger_price`, `average_price` | `price`, `trigger_price`, `trade_average_price` or `average_price` |
| `order_timestamp` | `order_date_time` or `order_timestamp`, India time, as ISO with the IST offset |
| `cancelled_quantity`, `exchange_timestamp` | null |
| `tag` | `tag` |

**Sessions.** The access token is read afresh for every authentication, so a login made by any process is picked up. An authentication refused with `AuthorizationError` logs in again through `StoxkartAPI` before retrying. Other failures reconnect with a backoff from 1 to 60 seconds.

Exits 0 when stopped with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM, and 1 when the first login, or a login after the session died, fails. Reads only - it never places, modifies or cancels an order.
"""

